  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. Its first run finished with status `ok`.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The data view \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\" holds web server access logs, using `timestamp` as the time field. The main fields are:\n- **Request details:** `response.keyword` (HTTP status code, stored as text), `bytes`, `url`/`request`, `extension`, `referer` and `agent`\n- **Who and where:** `clientip`, `geo.src`/`geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates` and `machine.os`\n- **Other:** `host`, `tags`, `memory`, and a computed `hour_of_day` field\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-6a4d9ad2 Web logs overview\"** (id `7a8e1383-81a4-4059-b94e-aee548463cef`, set to the last 7 days). It has six panels:\n- Total requests\n- Unique visitors (distinct `clientip`)\n- Average bytes per request\n- Error rate: the percentage of 4xx and 5xx responses, calculated with an ES|QL query\n- Requests over time, stacked by response code\n- A donut chart of requests by response code\n\nKibana accepted all six panels and they are saved on the dashboard, but I haven't opened it in the browser to check that they render. The error-rate panel is the one most worth a quick look.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-6a4d9ad2 5xx spike\"** (id `44b394da-fdb8-4f34-8e03-7ec274b6f1a4`):\n- It is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs` that runs every minute.\n- It counts documents whose `response.keyword` starts with \"5\" (any 5xx code) in the last 5 minutes and fires when the count is more than 10.\n- It has no actions attached, so when it fires it creates alerts but doesn't send notifications. Add a connector, such as email or Slack, if you want to be notified.\n\nThe request bodies I sent are saved in the scratchpad as `dash.json` and `rule.json`.",
